Z||000006||deceler8||07-31-2001||12:26 PM||||This seemed like one of those events you would never imagine happening, but the power of a 10 ton electromagnet which uses megawatts of power has to be awesome. <P>Seems like this power could turn even small objects into deadly projectiles...amazing this has apparently never happened before.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Pontiaddict||07-31-2001||01:49 PM||||That kind of thing sucks.<P><BR>My dad had a Grinding wheel blow up in his face once and he got a few metal filings in his eye. Actually some of them worked their way around to the back of his eye socket. He was fine after it happened but you had to know that before I told you this.<P><BR>Before the hopital let my dad had to go in for his MRI they had to take some x-rays and make sure they got the last of the metal filings out of his eyes before putting him in the machine. Imagine getting small shards of metal pulled through you.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Raydar||07-31-2001||03:57 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Pontiaddict:<BR><B>...Before the hopital let my dad had to go in for his MRI they had to take some x-rays and make sure they got the last of the metal filings out of his eyes before putting him in the machine. Imagine getting small shards of metal pulled through you.</B><BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>They were talking about this on one of the local talk radio stations, this morning. They have to make sure that there are no fragments of ferrous (magnetic) metal, anywhere in your body. They related a story about a guy who got shot in the forehead with a BB gun when he was a kid, and never told anyone about it. (Didn't want mom to yell at him.) Seems he went for an MRI, years later, and the machine pulled the BB <I>out through the skin on his forehead</I>.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 07-31-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Jaygee79||07-31-2001||06:04 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JohnnyK:<BR><B>The person who brought that in there should have nothing happen to him. It was a freak accident. I'm sure he feels terrible. I can't believe you think he should suffer consequences. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>They are told specifically not to let any metal objects into that area.  Why?  Because something like this could happen.  I'm not saying he should have his legs chopped off, but a suspension or getting fired maybe.<p>[This message has been edited by Jaygee79 (edited 07-31-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||07-31-2001||08:26 PM||||I've worked around those things... The ones used by DuPont are smaller but they will do major damage. They will screw up computer monitors 20 feet away. Will easily launch things up closer.<P>What amazes me was that the door to the rooom above apparently doesn't have some type of automatic lock to keep some fool from walkin in there when it's running. I don't blame the person with the tank as much as the people who set it up and maintain it.<P>Think about it a second... When all the doors on a hall look about the same it can be really easy to open the wrong one at a bad time. You can't count on a sign... when you work around so many of them they eventually become part of the wallpaper. They simply stop registering after awhile.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Pontiaddict||07-31-2001||08:52 PM||||If I remember right, hospitals are supposed to keep people with pacemakers and other electronically controlled equipment at least 2 floors away from MRI's too.||||reg||1
